          "text": "**My Approach to the Divisibility Problem**\n\nOkay, so the goal here is to find out how many integers between 1 and 100 are divisible by either 3 or 5, but *not* by both. I need to deliver a single number as the answer.\n\nFirst, I define my sets. I have the set *S* containing all integers from 1 to 100. Then I have the subset *A* containing numbers in *S* divisible by 3, and subset *B* containing numbers in *S* divisible by 5.  I calculate the cardinalities of *A* and *B*.  Floor(100/3) gives me 33 elements in *A*, and floor(100/5) gives me 20 elements in *B*.\n\nNext, I identify that numbers divisible by *both* 3 and 5 are divisible by their least common multiple, which is 15. The intersection of *A* and *B* contains these, so I compute its cardinality as floor(100/15), resulting in 6 elements in *A* intersect *B*.\n\nNow, the core of the problem is the symmetric difference.  I know the formula for the symmetric difference, which is |A Δ B| = |A| + |B| - 2|A ∩ B|.  Plugging in the numbers gives 33 + 20 - 2 * 6, which equals 41.\n\nTo be extra certain, I do a quick check to see if that answer holds up.  I can find the elements divisible by only 3 by calculating |A \\ B| which is 33-6, or 27. I then find elements divisible by only 5, and arrive at 20-6, or 14. These two numbers, 27 and 14, sum to 41. So, yes, that is correct.\n\nFinally, the output should be just the number.\n\n\n",
